  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Late leads don't survive careless late innings. Except when one swing erases the whole debate.",
    "what_this_means": "Tyler Mahle gave them six frames without damage. The bullpen moved through the back end cleanly: Hentges, Walker, Smith, Miller all accounted for, a run giving way but nothing permanent. By the eighth, the margin was gone. A run down with most of the game behind them. The ninth inning was where this team lived or died. Bericoto answered with a home run, and the game was suddenly theirs. One swing, one moment. That's how fragile wins feel when you're chasing this many games.",
    "what_to_watch": "This was a tied game that broke open late instead of a comeback. They never fell behind enough to claw back. They just lost the lead in eight and reclaimed it in nine. The thin margin is becoming the way they play now: Jung Hoo Lee hit too, the lineup scraped together what it needed. But Bericoto was the separating moment. In a game where nothing was decided until the ninth, one bat finishing it matters more than a pitcher's clean six. The offense didn't outscore Oakland. It outwaited them. Eight left on base earlier. None of it mattered in the end.",
